From: StealthOS TorClient <opensource@olib.ai>
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] time_fmt: use the arithmetic tor_timegm() on Apple platforms
Upstream-Status: candidate (not yet submitted)
Applies-To: tor 0.4.9.14

tor_timegm() calls the system timegm() when configure finds one. On Apple
platforms every timegm() goes through gmtsub() -> notify_check_tz(). On
macOS and iOS devices that check reads a shared-memory notify token, but in
the iOS simulator it is a Mach IPC round trip to the simulator's notifyd:
10000 x timegm() took 8.2 s in the iPhone simulator (iOS 27.1) and 33 ms
on the host macOS.

run_tor_main_loop() reloads the cached consensus before the event loop
starts, and routerstatus_parse_entry_from_string() parses one ISO time per
router status entry (parse_iso_time_() -> tor_timegm()), ~8000 calls. A
`sample` of the Tor thread during a restart shows 93% of its time in
router_reload_consensus_networkstatus -> ... -> parse_iso_time_ -> time2 ->
notify_check_tz -> mach_msg. So every in-process restart with a cached
consensus kept Tor out of its main loop for ~5 s (more under load). The
owning controller connection is not serviced until the loop runs, so a
GETINFO or SIGNAL right after start() timed out (StealthOS's 100-restart
stress test: GETINFO took 5.0-5.3 s on every cycle once the consensus was
cached, and failed its 10 s timeout under concurrent test load; SIGNAL HALT
in stop() ran into its 5 s timeout).

Use tor_timegm_impl(), the implementation Tor already uses where timegm()
is missing, on __APPLE__. Apple's time_t does not count leap seconds, so the
result is identical for every valid input; the only difference is that
out-of-range fields (e.g. April 31) are rejected instead of normalized, and
every caller passes fields it has range-checked or got from gmtime().
---
--- a/src/lib/encoding/time_fmt.c
+++ b/src/lib/encoding/time_fmt.c
@@ -170,7 +170,7 @@
 int
 tor_timegm(const struct tm *tm, time_t *time_out)
 {
-#ifdef HAVE_TIMEGM
+#if defined(HAVE_TIMEGM) && !defined(__APPLE__)
   /* If the system gives us a timegm(), use it: if the system's time_t
    * includes leap seconds, then we can hope that its timegm() knows too.
    *
@@ -188,7 +188,14 @@
     return 0;
   }
 #else
-  /* The system doesn't have timegm; we'll have to use our own. */
+  /* The system doesn't have timegm; we'll have to use our own.
+   *
+   * Apple platforms have timegm(), but every call goes through
+   * notify_check_tz(), which in the iOS simulator is a Mach IPC round trip
+   * to notifyd (about 0.8 ms). Loading a cached consensus calls this once
+   * per router status entry, so the main loop started seconds late and the
+   * control port did not answer meanwhile. Apple's time_t has no leap
+   * seconds, so the arithmetic implementation gives the same result. */
   return tor_timegm_impl(tm, time_out);
 #endif
 }
